```jsx
function TicketSearch({ query }) {
  const [state, setState] = useState({ status: 'idle', items: [], error: null });

  useEffect(() => {
    let current = true; // this run is the one that counts — until cleanup says otherwise
    setState((s) => ({ ...s, status: 'loading' }));

    searchTickets(query)
      .then((payload) => {
        if (!current) return; // a newer query has already been sent; drop this answer
        setState({ status: 'ready', items: payload.results.map(mapTicketResponse), error: null });
      })
      .catch((error) => {
        if (!current) return;
        setState({ status: 'error', items: [], error: error.message });
      });

    return () => {
      current = false; // runs before the next effect and on unmount
    };
  }, [query]);

  const view = selectListView({ ...state, query });
  return <QueueView view={view} />;
}
```

The `current` flag is the whole stale-response defence, and it is four lines. React runs the cleanup of the previous effect before starting the next one, so by the time a second request is sent the first run has already been marked as no longer wanted — its `.then` still runs, but it returns immediately instead of writing state.

Notice too that `mapTicketResponse` and `selectListView` are both plain functions. All the decision-making is outside the component, which is why both are runnable exercises below.

