```jsx
function QueueView({ view, onRetry, onClearFilter }) {
  // Failure first: an empty list during an outage must not read as 'no tickets'.
  if (view.view === 'error') {
    return <ErrorPanel message={view.message} onRetry={onRetry} />;
  }
  if (view.view === 'loading') return <QueueSkeleton />;
  if (view.view === 'no-matches') {
    return <NoMatches query={view.query} onClearFilter={onClearFilter} />;
  }
  if (view.view === 'empty') return <EmptyQueue />;

  return (
    <ul>
      {view.items.map((ticket) => (
        <li key={ticket.id}>
          {ticket.subject}
          {/* status carries a word, not just a colour */}
          <span className={`badge badge--${ticket.slaState}`}>
            {ticket.slaState === 'breached' ? 'SLA breached' : 'On track'}
          </span>
          {/* a real button, with a name that says which ticket */}
          <button type="button" aria-label={`Resolve ${ticket.subject}`} onClick={...}>
            <CheckIcon aria-hidden="true" />
          </button>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

The branch order is the design. Because `selectListView` already collapsed the state into one tag, this component cannot render a spinner and an empty state together, and there is no combination of flags that reaches the wrong branch.

In the row, `aria-hidden="true"` on the icon and `aria-label` on the button are a pair: the decorative graphic is hidden from the accessibility tree and the button supplies the name instead — and that name includes the subject, so a screen-reader user hearing twenty "Resolve" buttons can tell them apart.

